      "text": "To find out how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes, we start by determining her walking rate in miles per minute. We know that she walks one mile in 24 minutes. Therefore, her rate is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Rate} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how far she will walk in 10 minutes. We can do this by multiplying her rate by the number of minutes:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Rate} \\times \\text{Time} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nPerforming the multiplication gives:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{10}{24}\\), we divide both the numerator and the denominator by their greatest common divisor, which is 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{24} = \\frac{10 \\div 2}{24 \\div 2} = \\frac{5}{12}\n\\]\n\nWe now convert \\(\\frac{5}{12}\\) to a decimal. Performing the division:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{12} \\approx 0.416667\n\\]\n\nRounding this to the nearest tenth gives:\n\n\\[\n0.416667 \\approx 0.4\n\\]\n\nThus, Jana will walk approximately \\(\\boxed{0.4}\\) miles in 10 minutes.",
