      "text": "To determine how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes, we need to first find her walking speed in miles per minute. We know that it takes her 24 minutes to walk one mile. Therefore, her speed is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Speed} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} = \\frac{1}{24} \\text{ miles per minute}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how far she will walk in 10 minutes. We do this by multiplying her speed by the time she will walk:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} = \\left( \\frac{1}{24} \\text{ miles per minute} \\right) \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nPerforming the multiplication gives:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{10}{24}\\), we divide both the numerator and the denominator by their greatest common divisor, which is 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{24} = \\frac{10 \\div 2}{24 \\div 2} = \\frac{5}{12}\n\\]\n\nNow, we convert \\(\\frac{5}{12}\\) to a decimal. Performing the division, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{12} \\approx 0.416667\n\\]\n\nRounding this to the nearest tenth, we get:\n\n\\[\n0.416667 \\approx 0.4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Jana will walk \\(\\boxed{0.4}\\) miles in 10 minutes.",
